  <h1>India’s Defence Modernisation — Indigenous Capability and Strategic Needs</h1>
  <div><em>Economic Times | Date: 2024 • ~600 words</em></div>
  <h3>Introduction</h3><p>Defence modernisation is central to India’s national security strategy amid rising regional tensions and global power shifts. In 2024, Economic Times reported India’s focus on indigenous defence production, technology infusion, and strategic partnerships. For UPSC, this issue reflects the convergence of security, economy, and geopolitics.
________________________________________</p>
  <h3>Body</h3><div>________________________________________<br/>Introduction<br/><br/>Current Status<br/>•	Defence Budget (2024–25): ~₹6.2 lakh crore (~2% of GDP).<br/>•	Indigenisation Target: 75% of procurement from domestic sources by 2025.<br/>•	Arms Imports: India remains among top 3 global arms importers (SIPRI, 2023).<br/>•	DRDO Role: 50+ projects including missiles, UAVs, electronic warfare.<br/>•	Private Sector: Growing participation under “Make in India” in defence.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Key Challenges<br/>1.	Import Dependence: High reliance on Russia, US, France for critical tech.<br/>2.	Delayed Procurement: Long acquisition cycles and red tape.<br/>3.	R&D Gaps: Low defence R&D spend (~0.9% of GDP).<br/>4.	Technology Obsolescence: Outdated platforms in air force and navy.<br/>5.	Private Sector Constraints: Limited ecosystem for SMEs and startups.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Government Initiatives<br/>•	Defence Acquisition Procedure (DAP, 2020): Prioritises Indian vendors.<br/>•	Positive Indigenisation Lists: Ban on import of 400+ items.<br/>•	iDEX (Innovations for Defence Excellence): Promotes startups and R&D.<br/>•	Strategic Partnerships: Submarine, fighter jet, helicopter production with private firms.<br/>•	Export Push: Defence exports crossed ₹16,000 crore in 2023–24.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Achievements<br/>•	Missile Programs: Agni, Akash, BrahMos expansion.<br/>•	Naval Strengthening: Indigenous aircraft carrier INS Vikrant commissioned (2022).<br/>•	Aircraft: HAL Tejas inducted in IAF squadrons.<br/>•	Global Recognition: India exporting defence equipment to 85 countries.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Persistent Gaps<br/>•	Dependence on Foreign Engines & Electronics: Weak domestic ecosystem.<br/>•	Joint Ventures: Often limited to assembly, not full tech transfer.<br/>•	Operational Shortfalls: Air Force fighter strength below sanctioned levels.<br/>•	Cyber & Space Defence: Still nascent capacities.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Significance<br/>•	National Security: Modernisation vital for two-front threat scenario (China–Pakistan).<br/>•	Strategic Autonomy: Reduce vulnerability to supply shocks.<br/>•	Economic Growth: Defence sector can drive jobs, R&D, exports.<br/>•	Diplomacy: Defence partnerships as tools of strategic influence.<br/>________________________________________<br/>UPSC Relevance<br/>•	GS-III (Security & Economy): Defence modernisation, indigenisation.<br/>•	GS-II (IR): Strategic partnerships, diplomacy.<br/>•	Essay:<br/>o	“Self-reliance in defence is the cornerstone of sovereignty.”<br/>o	“India’s rise as a global power depends on its military strength.”<br/>•	PSIR Paper II: Security, realism, and defence politics.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Way Forward<br/>1.	Boost R&D: Raise defence R&D to 2% of GDP, focus on AI, drones, cyber.<br/>2.	Strengthen Private Sector: Incentives for MSMEs, tech startups.<br/>3.	Faster Procurement: Reduce delays, adopt transparent processes.<br/>4.	Global Collaborations: Balance imports with meaningful tech transfer.<br/>5.	Integrated Modernisation: Joint planning among Army, Navy, Air Force.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Conclusion</div>
  <h3>Conclusion</h3><p>India’s defence modernisation journey reflects the quest for self-reliance, security, and global influence. Success will depend on building indigenous ecosystems while leveraging strategic partnerships. For UPSC aspirants, it captures the intersection of security, technology, and geopolitics.</p>
  <div>Diagram Placeholder: Defence Modernisation Path — Import Dependence → Indigenisation → Export Growth</div>
